    "narrative": "The offensive engine for Ghost Ride the WHIP centers on **Corbin Carroll** (77.88) and **Josh Naylor** (65.02), our premier starts for today\u2019s slate. **Corbin Carroll** carries the highest efficiency score as he squares off against McGreevy; his elite speed profile, combined with the favorable +13% adjustment for the opposing SP\u2019s xERA, makes him a foundational play. **Josh Naylor** similarly benefits from a strong matchup against Chandler, anchored by a solid cleanup spot and a +5% SP skill adjustment. Meanwhile, **Gabriel Moreno** (61.48) and **Jose Caballero** (59.98) provide necessary stability. **Jose Caballero** is particularly noteworthy today; despite the unfavorable SP skill (-14%), the +13% platoon edge against an LHP and a +7% park factor boost make him a high-upside play for stealing bases, which remains our primary objective.\n\nDecisions in the outfield were tight, as **Tyler Soderstrom** (57.66) narrowly edged out the rest of the utility contingent due to his clean platoon advantage against Roupp. The most interesting bench decision involved the exclusion of our depth, as the system currently reflects zero availability for **Mickey Moniak**, **Trevor Story**, **Brice Turang**, and **Austin Riley** due to off-days or injury. Given these constraints, the lineup is optimized to maximize plate appearances in the top half of the order. We are proceeding with caution regarding the pending statuses of **Gabriel Moreno**, **James Wood**, **Seiya Suzuki**, and **Corbin Carroll**; should these confirmed batting orders shift lower, we may see a regression in the efficiency scores for **James Wood** (51.16) and **Seiya Suzuki** (54.35), particularly given the latter\u2019s challenge against the high-velocity arm of Freddy Peralta. We will continue to monitor weather and late-scratch news to ensure our active roster remains as aggressive as possible to chase that elusive stolen base category.",
